> Weight-related conditions such as high blood pressure, sleep apnoea and joint pain may change after surgery for some patients, but outcomes vary and ongoing medical care may still be needed.

Source: extracted draft L208-L210.

> Dumping syndrome; feeling unwell after eating sugary or fatty foods. Dumping syndrome usually improves as eating habits adjust.

> Dumping syndrome can cause symptoms after eating sugary or fatty foods, and symptoms may change as eating habits adjust. Your dietitian will explain what to watch for and when to seek advice.

Source: extracted draft L251-L255.

> On average, gastric bypass patients lose around 70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.

> Clinical averages are around 70–80% of excess body weight over 12–18 months, but individual results vary by starting weight, health, nutrition, activity and follow-up. Your surgeon can discuss a realistic expectation for your circumstances without promising a particular result.

Source: extracted draft L291-L297.

