   246	### <span class="mark">FAQs (8 questions)</span>
   247	
   248	1.  **<span class="mark">How much weight will I lose after a gastric
   249	    bypass?</span>**
   250	
   251	<span class="mark">On average, gastric bypass patients lose around
   252	70–80% of their excess body weight over 12–18 months (slightly more than
   253	the sleeve on average). Individual results vary based on starting
   254	weight, age, diet, and activity levels, and your surgeon will give you a
   255	realistic expectation based on your individual circumstances.</span>
   256	
   257	2.  **<span class="mark">Will I get loose skin?</span>**
   258	
   259	<span class="mark">Loose skin is common after significant weight loss.
   260	How much loose skin your weight loss results in depends on your age,
   261	your genetics, your skin elasticity, and of course, how much weight you
   262	lose. Exercise, particularly resistance training, can help with body
   263	composition during your weight loss phase. You might look into plastic
   264	surgery for loose skin (‘body contouring’ and ‘skin removal’) as an
   265	option down the track, though bear in mind it is not covered by the same
   266	funding pathways.</span>
   267	
   268	3.  **<span class="mark">How long will I need off work?</span>**
   269	
   270	<span class="mark">Most patients require around 2-3 weeks off desk-based
   271	or sedentary work. Physical or manual labour typically requires closer
   272	to 6-8 weeks before returning. Everyone recovers at a different pace,
   273	though, and your surgeon will provide guidance tailored to your
   274	situation. It's worth factoring recovery time into your planning early,
   275	particularly if your role involves lifting, physical exertion, or
   276	extended periods on your feet.</span>
   277	
   278	4.  **<span class="mark">What if it doesn't work for me?</span>**
   279	
   280	<span class="mark">A gastric bypass is one of the most effective
   281	bariatric procedures available, but results depend on a combination of
   282	surgery and sustained lifestyle change. If weight loss is slower than
   283	expected or regain occurs over time, the first step is reviewing diet,
   284	habits, and follow-up care with your team. Further surgical options may
   285	be considered in some cases. Your surgeon and dietitian are there to
   286	support you through challenges, not just the initial recovery.</span>
   287	
   288	5.  **<span class="mark">Can I eat normally/what can I never eat after a
   289	    gastric bypass?</span>**
   290	
   291	<span class="mark">The great news is that most foods can be reintroduced
   292	over time. But ‘normal’ does look different after a bypass. Portions
   293	will be much smaller, eating too quickly (or too much) can cause
   294	discomfort, and some patients experience dumping syndrome — a reaction
   295	to high-sugar/high-fat foods that can cause nausea, cramping, and
   296	lightheadedness. Most people adapt well though and many find their
   297	relationship with food improves meaningfully over time.</span>
   298	
   299	6.  **<span class="mark">Is gastric bypass better than a gastric
   300	    sleeve?</span>**
   301	
   302	<span class="mark">Neither procedure is universally better. The bypass
   303	tends to produce slightly greater weight loss and has stronger outcomes
   304	for type 2 diabetes and acid reflux, but it is more complex with greater
   305	nutritional demands long-term. The sleeve is less invasive and leaves
   306	your digestion intact. The right choice is an individual one, best made
   307	in consultation with your surgeon after a thorough assessment of your
   308	health history and goals.</span>
   309	
   310	7.  **<span class="mark">Do I need a GP referral?</span>**
   311	
   312	<span class="mark">A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book
   313	a consultation, but having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates
   314	to apply to your specialist consultation fee, reducing your
   315	out-of-pocket cost for the appointment. It also ensures your surgeon has
   316	your relevant medical history from the outset. If you don't yet have a
   317	referral, our team can advise you on next steps.</span>
   318	
   319	8.  **<span class="mark">Where is the surgery performed?</span>**
   320	
   321	<span class="mark">The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric
   322	bypass surgery in Perth at three locations: [<u>Mount Private
   323	Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private
   324	Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [<u>Waikiki Private
   325	Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Pre- and post-operative
   326	(once you’ve been discharged from hospital) consultations take place at
   327	Garden City Specialist Centre, Booragoon, and WA Cardiology Centre in
   328	Greenfields.</span>
