+> Neither procedure is universally better. Gastric bypass may be discussed for some patients with type 2 diabetes or reflux, but it is more complex and requires lifelong nutritional follow-up; a gastric sleeve may be appropriate for others. Read our [gastric sleeve Perth guide](/gastric-sleeve-perth) and [bariatric surgery Perth overview](/bariatric-surgery-perth), then discuss suitability, risks and expected outcomes with your surgeon.
+
+> **Compress the loose-skin FAQ answer to two sentences. Source: extracted draft L259-L266.**
+>
+> Loose skin is common after significant weight loss. How much loose skin your weight loss results in depends on your age, your genetics, your skin elasticity, and of course, how much weight you lose. Exercise, particularly resistance training, can help with body composition during your weight loss phase. You might look into plastic surgery for loose skin (‘body contouring’ and ‘skin removal’) as an option down the track, though bear in mind it is not covered by the same funding pathways.
+>
+> Loose skin can occur after significant weight loss, and the amount varies with factors such as age, genetics, skin elasticity and the amount of weight lost. Some patients discuss body-contouring or skin-removal surgery after their weight has stabilised; funding and eligibility differ from bariatric surgery, so confirm any coverage with your surgeon and insurer.
+
+> **Compress the time-off-work FAQ answer to two sentences. Source: extracted draft L270-L276.**
+>
+> Most patients require around 2-3 weeks off desk-based or sedentary work. Physical or manual labour typically requires closer to 6-8 weeks before returning. Everyone recovers at a different pace, though, and your surgeon will provide guidance tailored to your situation. It's worth factoring recovery time into your planning early, particularly if your role involves lifting, physical exertion, or extended periods on your feet.
+>
+> Many people need around 2–3 weeks away from desk-based work, while physical or manual work may require longer. Your surgeon will advise based on your recovery and job demands, especially if your role involves lifting, physical exertion or prolonged periods on your feet.
+
+> **Compress the “what if it doesn't work” FAQ answer to two sentences. Source: extracted draft L280-L286.**
+>
+> A gastric bypass is one of the most effective bariatric procedures available, but results depend on a combination of surgery and sustained lifestyle change. If weight loss is slower than expected or regain occurs over time, the first step is reviewing diet, habits, and follow-up care with your team. Further surgical options may be considered in some cases. Your surgeon and dietitian are there to support you through challenges, not just the initial recovery.
+>
+> Gastric bypass can be an effective bariatric procedure for suitable patients, but results depend on surgery, follow-up care, nutrition, activity and long-term behaviour change. If weight loss is slower than expected or regain occurs, your team can review your diet, symptoms and follow-up plan; further assessment or revision surgery may be considered in selected cases.
 
+> **Compress the eating FAQ answer to two sentences. Source: extracted draft L291-L297.**
+>
+> The great news is that most foods can be reintroduced over time. But ‘normal’ does look different after a bypass. Portions will be much smaller, eating too quickly (or too much) can cause discomfort, and some patients experience dumping syndrome — a reaction to high-sugar/high-fat foods that can cause nausea, cramping, and lightheadedness. Most people adapt well though and many find their relationship with food improves meaningfully over time.
+>
+> Most foods can be reintroduced gradually, but portions are smaller and eating too quickly or too much can cause discomfort. Some patients experience dumping syndrome after high-sugar or high-fat foods; your dietitian will guide food reintroduction and the long-term eating plan.
+
+> **Compress the GP-referral FAQ answer to two sentences. Source: extracted draft L312-L317.**
+>
+> A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book a consultation, but having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates to apply to your specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the appointment. It also ensures your surgeon has your relevant medical history from the outset. If you don't yet have a referral, our team can advise you on next steps.
+>
+> A referral is not required to make an enquiry or book a consultation. A valid referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to Medicare rules; it does not guarantee a rebate or cover the full fee. It can also help your surgeon receive relevant medical history, and the team can advise you if you do not yet have one.
+
+> **Compress the surgery-location FAQ answer to two sentences. Source: extracted draft L321-L328.**
+>
+> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at three locations: [Mount Private Hospital](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [The Park Private Hospital](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [Waikiki Private Hospital](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Pre- and post-operative (once you’ve been discharged from hospital) consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre, Booragoon, and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
+>
+> The Obesity Surgery WA surgical team performs gastric bypass surgery in Perth at the hospital location(s) confirmed by your surgeon. Pre- and post-operative consultations take place at Garden City Specialist Centre in Booragoon and WA Cardiology Centre in Greenfields.
+
+> Keep the Medicare/private-health detail on `/medicare-private-health-cover`.
